# ============================================================
# PROLOGUE — "OVERBURDEN"
# 2026. The dig at Dholarath, first light. ~20 minutes.
# ============================================================
#
# DRAFT BY CLAUDE — from docs/kaalchakra-story-bible.md §5. Yours to cut.
# Places where the draft made a creative call are marked  # ?? DECISION
#
# STRUCTURE: the scene alternates talking and playing. `@ handover` stops the
# dialogue, hands control back, and waits until Eva reaches somewhere or
# finishes something — then the scene picks up again. Without that it is one
# unbroken wall of text with a frozen camera.
#
# ---- how to write in this file -----------------------------------------
#   #     a note. Ignored.
#   ::    starts a chunk
#   NAME: who is speaking. A bare indented line continues the one above,
#         so a long speech is still ONE beat and ONE voice file.
#   (..)  stage direction — for the actor, and for me
#   >     a choice the player taps
#   ->    jump to another chunk
#   @     an instruction to me
#
# @ handover <flag> <chunk>   stop talking; resume at <chunk> when <flag> is set
# @ camera move_to <marker>   send the camera to a named shot
# @ camera push_in <marker>   same, slower and closer
# @ camera release            give the camera back to the player
#
# Camera markers that exist: trench, sealed_door, chamber, manifest_wall,
# keeper, wheel, north_gate, signboard, reservoir.
# Flags the world can set: at_trench_top, at_trench_bottom, in_chamber,
# chamber_opened.
#
# If you want something that isn't listed, write it anyway. The format bends
# to the story, not the other way round.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------


:: start
@ era present
@ camera release
@ music bansuri_sparse
# She is filming on her phone, down on the basin floor. The player has control
# from the first word — no opening cutscene.
EVA: (to the phone, quiet, the whole site still asleep) Day nineteen.
EVA: Sun's not up. Ishita's not up. The monsoon is three weeks early and nobody
    at the Institute wants to talk about what that does to a trench.
EVA: (beat) But you lot didn't come here for drainage.
@ objective "Walk up to Trench 4"
@ handover at_trench_top the_pitch


:: the_pitch
# Standing over the trench, looking down into it.
@ camera move_to trench
EVA: (turning the camera to the pit) You came for this.
EVA: Dholarath. Four and a half thousand years old. Grid streets, covered
    drains, a reservoir that could hold a season of rain —
EVA: — and a writing system that nobody on Earth can read.
EVA: Four hundred signs. Thousands of inscriptions. Not one of them understood.
# ?? DECISION — her mother's theory, this early. It is the engine of the whole
# game and I wanted it heard before anything impossible happens.
EVA: Thirty years ago somebody worked out why. She said: stop reading it like a
    language. It isn't sentences. It's an index — labels, pointing at something
    that isn't the writing.
EVA: The field laughed her out of the room.
EVA: (flat) She's my mother. So, you know. Personal stake.
@ objective "Climb down into the trench"
@ camera release
@ handover at_trench_bottom teach_dig


:: teach_dig
# EXCAVATION TAUGHT HERE, on something worthless, so failing costs nothing.
@ skill_unlock excavation
EVA: (settling in) Right. Slowly. We don't take, we record.
# ?? DECISION — her father's trowel, before the chamber rather than after, so
# the discovery is not the first time the player hears about him.
EVA: (almost ritual) Brush, don't scrape. Dad's rule.
@ sfx trowel_on_pottery
EVA: (genuine delight) Oh, hello.
EVA: (holding it up to the lens) Potsherd. Red slip. Probably a storage jar,
    probably nothing, probably the single most beautiful nothing you'll see today.
-> ishita_arrives


:: ishita_arrives
ISHITA: (above, dry) Two million people and you still can't operate a kettle.
EVA: Two million people fund your remote sensing, Dr Vaid.
ISHITA: Two million people think you found a lost city. You found a
    well-documented site with a new trench in it.
EVA: Two million people have hope. You should try it.
> "Come down and look at the floor."   -> ishita_down   @ flag ishita_invited
> "Go back to bed."                    -> ishita_stays
> (say nothing, keep working)          -> ishita_stays


:: ishita_down
@ flag ishita_present_at_discovery
ISHITA: (sliding down, landing badly) ...What am I looking at?
EVA: That's what I'm asking you. You're the one with the ground scans.
-> call_or_work


:: ishita_stays
ISHITA: (fading, above) When you find Atlantis, wake me.
EVA: (to herself) Rude.
-> call_or_work


:: call_or_work
@ phone_available sakshi
# First meeting with the hint system. Strongly cued, never forced.
> Call Ma                              -> call_sakshi
> Keep working                         -> the_lintel


:: call_sakshi
@ sfx phone_dial
@ music sakshi_theme
SAKSHI: (crackly line, instantly awake) You're in the trench.
EVA: How do you always —
SAKSHI: Because it's five in the morning and you're whispering.
EVA: (caught) Ma.
SAKSHI: What did you find?
EVA: Red slip sherd. And the floor's wrong. It's too flat, and it's too flat in
    a straight line.
SAKSHI: (a long pause) Check the drainage, beta. Not the walls. Everyone always
    checks the walls.
SAKSHI: I told the reviewers that in '98 and they laughed at me for an hour.
# ?? DECISION — "beta" and "Ma". Your call on the register, and how much
# Gujarati or Hindi threads through. I defaulted to the lightest touch.
EVA: (quiet) I know, Ma.
SAKSHI: Don't "I know" me. Go and look at the drain.
@ flag called_sakshi_prologue
-> the_lintel


:: the_lintel
# The find. She stops performing for the camera, which is the tell.
@ music drop_out
@ sfx brush_on_stone
@ camera push_in sealed_door
EVA: (still presenting) Okay, so this is — this is probably a kerb stone, these
    run along the —
EVA: (stops)
EVA: That's a lintel.
ISHITA: [if ishita_present_at_discovery] (very quietly) Eva. That's a door.
EVA: [if ishita_present_at_discovery] I know it's a door.
ISHITA: [if ishita_present_at_discovery] That's a *sealed* door.
EVA: (not to the phone any more) Ma was right.
@ music low_drone_build
@ knowledge keeper_mark
@ objective "Clear the doorway"
@ camera release
# ?? DECISION — no fail state here. The first real discovery of the game should
# not be something you can botch. Failure starts in Chapter 2.
@ handover chamber_opened the_threshold


:: the_threshold
@ sfx stone_grinding
@ camera move_to chamber
@ music silence
EVA: (barely audible) ...Oh.
EVA: (to nobody) There's someone in here.
EVA: Sitting up. Not buried — placed. Facing the door.
@ objective "Go inside"
@ camera release
@ handover in_chamber the_wall


:: the_wall
@ camera move_to manifest_wall
EVA: And the whole back wall is covered in signs.
EVA: I can't read any of it. Nobody can read any of it.
EVA: (moving closer) Except —
EVA: Except that pairing. There, and there, and — six times.
EVA: That's the pairing off the north gate signboard. The one every tourist
    photograph in Gujarat has in the background.
EVA: (the thought arriving) It's a label. It's labelling something.
EVA: It's an index.
@ flag saw_the_index
-> the_keeper


:: the_keeper
@ camera push_in keeper
EVA: (quiet) Four thousand years sitting up in the dark, facing the way out.
EVA: Whoever you were, you weren't hiding. You were waiting.
-> the_wheel


:: the_wheel
@ camera push_in wheel
EVA: (down to the hands) And you're holding something.
EVA: Copper. Disc. Eight spokes.
EVA: (the archaeologist and the daughter arguing in one sentence) I should not
    touch this. I should photograph this, and log this, and call it in, and not
    touch this.
> Lift it                              -> lift
> Photograph it first                  -> photograph


:: photograph
@ sfx phone_shutter
@ flag recorded_before_taking
# ?? DECISION — her creed, "we don't take, we record", set as a real flag. I
# want it to pay off in Chapter 5 when Dhillon argues she is no different.
EVA: (steadier) Recorded. Logged. Witnessed.
EVA: Okay.
-> lift


:: lift
@ sfx low_hum_begin
EVA: (lifting it) It's warm.
EVA: (confused, quieter) Why is it warm —
@ camera whip_around
@ hard_cut
@ era past
-> arrival


:: arrival
# One frame 2026, the next 1900 BCE. Brick-new walls, water thundering
# somewhere above, and a city that is extremely alive.
@ sfx water_roar
@ music dholarath_alive
EVA: (no breath in her) What.
VASUDHA: (unimpressed, holding a plumb line) Late.
VASUDHA: You Keepers are always late.
@ title_card "KAALCHAKRA"
@ camera release
-> END
